PHASE 9 — VIEWER OPENING AND CLOSING MOTION
==================================================

Preserve/refine the requested motion:

OPEN:
The viewer rises smoothly from the bottom.

CLOSE:
The viewer moves smoothly back down.

Suggested duration:

Approximately 400–500ms, adjusted to the current animation system.

Do not unmount the viewer before the exit animation finishes.

Handle:

- Repeated clicks.
- Closing during opening.
- Opening a project again.
- Red close button.
- Regular close/back control.
- Escape where appropriate.

Use one consistent state model so controls cannot leave the viewer stuck halfway open.

Keep the BlueMind page stationary underneath the open modal viewer.

Keep the viewer toolbar visible while the project content scrolls.

Use appropriate dialog semantics and focus management.

For Escape, dismiss an active inner demo dialog first when appropriate, rather than unexpectedly closing the entire BlueMind viewer.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — ALTERNATING SCROLL-REVEAL ANIMATION
==================================================

Add an elegant reveal sequence as the visitor scrolls into Website Concepts.

SECTION INTRODUCTION:

The section label, heading, and short description can appear with:

- A small upward movement.
- A gentle opacity transition.

PROJECT CARDS:

Alternate the entry direction:

First card:
Enters from the RIGHT.

Second card:
Enters from the LEFT.

Third card:
From the RIGHT.

Fourth card:
From the LEFT.

Continue consistently through all seven cards.

Use a restrained movement, not a full-screen slide.

Suggested horizontal offset:

Approximately 24–40px on larger screens.

Suggested duration:

Approximately 450–600ms.

If a row contains two cards, use a small stagger between them.

Do not create a long accumulated delay that makes later cards wait several seconds.

Each card should animate when it approaches or enters the viewport.

Do not animate all seven immediately on page load while most are offscreen.

On mobile, use a smaller offset if needed to avoid distracting movement and clipping.

The effect should communicate:

“As I scroll, more concepts are introduced.”

NOT:

“The page is constantly moving around.”

==================================================
PHASE 11 — ANIMATION SAFETY AND COMPOSITION
==================================================

Keep reveal animation and hover animation on separate layers where necessary.

For example:

Outer wrapper:
Scroll reveal.

Inner interactive card:
Hover lift.

Preview layer:
Blur.

CTA layer:
Sharp and readable.

Do not allow one transform to overwrite another.

Prevent horizontal overflow caused by offscreen reveal positions.

Content must not remain permanently invisible if an observer or animation fails.

Respect reduced-motion preferences:

- Remove large translations.
- Avoid animated blur.
- Use immediate visibility or a short, simple transition.
- Keep every interaction available.

Do not replay all reveal animations when the visitor closes a demo and returns to the same scroll position.

Do not add sound effects.

